A program that names no standard sees SIGSTKSZ, MINSIGSTKSZ, SS_ONSTACK
and SS_DISABLE under _DEFAULT_SOURCE, as it sees the sigaltstack(2) they
go with, and as on glibc and musl. mlibc showed them only to a program
that defined _XOPEN_SOURCE, so C code that sets up a signal stack of its
own failed to compile, as the cgo tests in Go's suite did. Meant for
upstream.

--- a/abis/linux/signal.h
+++ b/abis/linux/signal.h
@@ -163,7 +163,7 @@
 #define SIGCANCEL 32
 #define SIGTIMER  33
 
-#if __MLIBC_XOPEN
+#if defined(_DEFAULT_SOURCE) || __MLIBC_XOPEN
 
 #if defined(__x86_64__) || defined(__i386__) || defined(__riscv) || defined(__m68k__)
 #define MINSIGSTKSZ 2048
